{{/*
  THE OS SWITCHBOARD.

  This is the only file in the repository that knows about the platform
  split. Everything else is a plain file that its own editor highlights and
  a human can grep. When you want to know why something did or did not land
  on a machine, the answer is here and nowhere else.

  Three rules that differ from .gitignore and will bite otherwise:

    1. Patterns match the TARGET path, never the source path.
       Write `.zshrc`, never `dot_zshrc`.
    2. `!` re-includes take priority over ALL ignores regardless of order.
       This is not git's last-match-wins.
    3. Always forward slashes, including for Windows targets.

  This file is a template even without a .tmpl suffix.

  To see what a different machine would do, from any machine:
    chezmoi ignored --override-data '{"chezmoi":{"os":"windows"}}'

  GOTCHA WORTH KNOWING: ignoring a file does NOT remove one that was already
  applied. The orphan then becomes invisible to `chezmoi managed`, `status`
  and `verify`, so continuous integration reports clean while stale config
  sits on disk. To actually delete something, use a `remove_` entry.
*/}}

{{- $isDarwin  := eq .chezmoi.os "darwin" -}}
{{- $isWindows := eq .chezmoi.os "windows" -}}

{{/* ---------- repository infrastructure ----------------------------------
  .chezmoiroot already scopes chezmoi to home/, so nothing at the repository
  root can reach a home directory. These entries are belt and braces for the
  case where someone runs chezmoi against the repository root by mistake.
*/}}
README.md
LICENSE
Makefile
install.sh
.github/**
docs/**
packages/**
scripts/**

{{ if not $isDarwin }}
{{/* ---------- macOS only ---------------------------------------------- */}}
Library/**
.config/terminal/**
.hushlogin
{{ end }}

{{ if $isWindows }}
{{/* ---------- everything POSIX, off on Windows -------------------------
  zsh does not exist on native Windows. PowerShell is the shell there and
  shares nothing with these files beyond the tool choices they configure.
  The ssh multiplexing directory goes too: Win32-OpenSSH has no Unix
  domain socket multiplexing, so ~/.ssh/cm would be a dead directory.
*/}}
.zshenv
.zprofile
.zshrc
.zsh_plugins.zsh
.config/zsh/**
.config/sh/**
.ssh/cm/**
{{ end }}

{{ if not $isWindows }}
{{/* ---------- Windows only --------------------------------------------
  The PowerShell profile is deployed to a stable path under .config and a
  one line shim is written at the real $PROFILE location by a script,
  because OneDrive Known Folder Move can relocate Documents by policy and
  chezmoi has no target path templating to follow it.
*/}}
.config/powershell/**
AppData/**
Documents/**
{{ end }}
